    ($SATQ$585a9138$SATQ$, $SATQ$715c4c96-5aac-4b8c-9adb-e5f7c886e5e6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Triangles <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper D upper E upper F"><mrow>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
	<mi>F</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are congruent, where <math alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi>
</math> corresponds to <math alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi>
</math>, and <math alttext="upper B"><mi>B</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> are right angles. The measure of angle <math alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="27 degree"><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle <math alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["63"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["63"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="63"><mn>63</mn></math>. It's given that triangles <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D upper E upper F"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math> are congruent with angle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi></math> corresponding to angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi></math>. Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are congruent and, therefore, have equal measure. It's given that the measure of&nbsp;angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="27 degree"><mn>27</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi></math> is also <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="27 degree"><mn>27</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi></math> is a right angle. Therefore, the measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="90 degree"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Let&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi></math>. Since the measures of the angles in a triangle sum to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="27 plus 90 plus x equals 180"><mn>27</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="117 plus x equals 180"><mn>117</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="117"><mn>117</mn></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 63"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn></math>. Therefore, the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="63"><mn>63</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"585a9138","external_id":"715c4c96-5aac-4b8c-9adb-e5f7c886e5e6","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"d56c9e2b-70f7-4bbd-8c41-f3be5b94c8f1","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Triangles <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are congruent, where <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> corresponds to <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math>, and <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> are right angles. The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"27 degree\"><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, 