les in a triangle sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"27 plus 90 plus x equals 180\"><mn>27</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"117 plus x equals 180\"><mn>117</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"117\"><mn>117</mn></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 63\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn></math>. Therefore, the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"63\"><mn>63</mn></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1776362727992,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"585a9138","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1776362727992,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"715c4c96-5aac-4b8c-9adb-e5f7c886e5e6","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"d56c9e2b-70f7-4bbd-8c41-f3be5b94c8f1","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Triangles <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are congruent, where <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> corresponds to <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math>, and <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> are right angles. The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"27 degree\"><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["63"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"63\"><mn>63</mn></math>. It's given that triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D upper E upper F\"><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></math> are congruent with angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> corresponding to angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math>. Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are congruent and, therefore, have equal measure. It's given that the measure of&nbsp;angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"27 degree\"><mn>27</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi></math> is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"27 degree\"><mn>27</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It's given that angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math> is a right angle. Therefore, the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"90 degree\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math>. Since the measures of the angles in a triangle sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"27 plus 90 plus x equals 180\"><mn>27</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"117 plus x equals 180\"><mn>117</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"117\"><mn>117</mn></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 63\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn></math>. Therefore, the measure, in degrees, of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"63\"><mn>63</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"715c4c96-5aac-4b8c-9adb-e5f7c886e5e6","correct_answer":["63"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$58c26db8$SATQ$, $SATQ$af8e7cb1-d233-45ae-8811-379283e1e3a4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The perimeter of an isosceles right triangle is <math alttext="18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mrow>
	<mn>18</mn>
	<mo>+</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>18</mn>
		<msqrt>
			<mn>2</mn>
		</msqrt>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this triangle?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2d17fa03-c80f-4309-92ca-6bcab014d6ce","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0af1e4e5-c1b6-4134-85c2-00759941d410","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8d585e08-9c28-4597-a9b2-4e89bc9a304a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is correct. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides. Since the given triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the length of each leg is the same and the length of the hypotenuse is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> times the length of a leg. Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle. Therefore, the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x plus x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, which is equivalent to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math>. It's given that the perimeter of this triangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches. Thus, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis equals 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mi>x</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals StartFraction 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the right-hand side of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals StartFraction 36 plus 36 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 36 Over 2 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. It follows that the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis left parenthesis StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="18"><mn>18</mn></math>. &nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="9 plus 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of a leg of this triangle, not the hypotenuse.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="36 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot"><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"58c26db8","external_id":"af8e7cb1-d233-45ae-8811-379283e1e3a4","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"325d3046-57da-4a42-90d9-d543ffe27745","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The perimeter of an isosceles right triangle is <math alttext=\"18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this triangle?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2d17fa03-c80f-4309-92ca-6bcab014d6ce","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0af1e4e5-c1b6-4134-85c2-00759941d410","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8d585e08-9c28-4597-a9b2-4e89bc9a304a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides. Since the given triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the length of each leg is the same and the length of the hypotenuse is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> times the length of a leg. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle. Therefore, the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x plus x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, which is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math>. It's given that the perimeter of this triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches. Thus, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis equals 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the right-hand side of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 36 plus 36 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 36 Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. It follows that the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis left parenthesis StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math>. &nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 plus 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of a leg of this triangle, not the hypotenuse.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"36 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017722,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"58c26db8","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1755117017722,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"af8e7cb1-d233-45ae-8811-379283e1e3a4","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"325d3046-57da-4a42-90d9-d543ffe27745","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The perimeter of an isosceles right triangle is <math alttext=\"18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t\t<msqrt>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msqrt>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> inches. What is the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this triangle?</p>","keys":["91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"2d17fa03-c80f-4309-92ca-6bcab014d6ce","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0af1e4e5-c1b6-4134-85c2-00759941d410","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"91b54cb1-0e67-4b6f-a31a-5d7e3367050f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"8d585e08-9c28-4597-a9b2-4e89bc9a304a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n\t<msqrt>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t</msqrt>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is correct. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides. Since the given triangle is an isosceles right triangle, the length of each leg is the same and the length of the hypotenuse is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> times the length of a leg. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle. Therefore, the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x plus x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 x plus x StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>, which is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mi>x</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>)</mo></math>. It's given that the perimeter of this triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches. Thus, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x left parenthesis 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis equals 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 18 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 plus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the right-hand side of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot Over 2 minus StartRoot 2 EndRoot EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 36 plus 36 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot minus 36 Over 2 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>-</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. It follows that the length, in inches, of a leg of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>. Therefore, the length, in inches, of the hypotenuse of this isosceles right triangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis left parenthesis StartRoot 2 EndRoot right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math>. &nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 plus 9 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>9</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect. This is the length, in inches, of a leg of this triangle, not the hypotenuse.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. If this were the length of the hypotenuse, the perimeter would be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"36 plus 18 StartRoot 2 EndRoot\"><mn>36</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>18</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math> inches.</p>","externalid":"af8e7cb1-d233-45ae-8811-379283e1e3a4","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1755117017722,"updateDate":1755117017722}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017722, 1755117017722),
    ($SATQ$59cb654c$SATQ$, $SATQ$4a39e52e-02ea-49d7-a695-87a667bdb785$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$09e6e2de-28aa-4a62-ae1e-66e8e5a554ce$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The area of a square is <math alttext="64"><mn>64</mn>
</math> square inches. What is the side length, in inches, of this square?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"6dc58e67-93bd-4e14-b40e-491b7e9f3157","content":"<p><math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"fdd5f441-5561-4fe1-87fa-677ce2e6ffbe","content":"<p><math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"35af31a2-ef6a-4019-9d48-541e8c56c204","content":"<p><math alttext=\"128\"><mn>128</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is correct. It's given that the area of a square is <math alttext="64"><mn>64</mn>
</math> square inches. The area <math alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi>
</math>, in square inches, of a square is given by the formula <math alttext="upper A equals s squared"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math alttext="s"><mi>s</mi>
</math> is the side length, in inches, of the square. Substituting <math alttext="64"><mn>64</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="upper A"><mi>A</mi>
</math> in this formula yields <math alttext="64 equals s squared"><mn>64</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext="8 equals s"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></math>. Thus, the side length, in inches, of this square is <math alttext="8"><mn>8</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the area, in square inches, of the square, not the side length, in inches, of the square.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"59cb654c","external_id":"4a39e52e-02ea-49d7-a695-87a667bdb785","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"09e6e2de-28aa-4a62-ae1e-66e8e5a554ce","uId":"1b3ef53d-5955-43a6-8a0b-25caea2d569c","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The area of a square is <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> square inches. What is the side length, in inches, of this square?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"6dc58e67-93bd-4e14-b40e-491b7e9f3157","content":"<p><math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"fdd5f441-5561-4fe1-87fa-677ce2e6ffbe","content":"<p><math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"35af31a2-ef6a-4019-9d48-541e8c56c204","content":"<p><math alttext=\"128\"><mn>128</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. It's given that the area of a square is <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> square inches. The area <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math>, in square inches, of a square is given by the formula <math alttext=\"upper A equals s squared\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math alttext=\"s\"><mi>s</mi>\n</math> is the side length, in inches, of the square. Substituting <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> in this formula yields <math alttext=\"64 equals s squared\"><mn>64</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext=\"8 equals s\"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></math>. Thus, the side length, in inches, of this square is <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the area, in square inches, of the square, not the side length, in inches, of the square.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959836,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"59cb654c","skill_cd":"S.A.","score_band_range_cd":1,"skill_desc":"Area and volume","createDate":1691007959836,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"4a39e52e-02ea-49d7-a695-87a667bdb785","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"1b3ef53d-5955-43a6-8a0b-25caea2d569c","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is correct. It's given that the area of a square is <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> square inches. The area <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math>, in square inches, of a square is given by the formula <math alttext=\"upper A equals s squared\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, where <math alttext=\"s\"><mi>s</mi>\n</math> is the side length, in inches, of the square. Substituting <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> in this formula yields <math alttext=\"64 equals s squared\"><mn>64</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext=\"8 equals s\"><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></math>. Thus, the side length, in inches, of this square is <math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the area, in square inches, of the square, not the side length, in inches, of the square.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The area of a square is <math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math> square inches. What is the side length, in inches, of this square?</p>","externalid":"4a39e52e-02ea-49d7-a695-87a667bdb785","templateid":"ed6f10d9-a147-44c2-80dd-7132ce30da4f","vaultid":"09e6e2de-28aa-4a62-ae1e-66e8e5a554ce","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"6bf174fa-7347-4bbd-b157-d6c8d34b05a4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"6dc58e67-93bd-4e14-b40e-491b7e9f3157","content":"<p><math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"fdd5f441-5561-4fe1-87fa-677ce2e6ffbe","content":"<p><math alttext=\"64\"><mn>64</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"35af31a2-ef6a-4019-9d48-541e8c56c204","content":"<p><math alttext=\"128\"><mn>128</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["A"]},"createDate":1691007959836,"updateDate":1691007959836}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959836, 1691007959836),
    ($SATQ$5a7e3b46$SATQ$, $SATQ$aa7ee350-2124-4fd5-afe1-f10a1f785d05$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">In <math alttext="triangle upper A upper B upper C"><mo>&#9651;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math alttext="angle upper B"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> is a right angle and the length of&nbsp;<math alttext="line segment upper B upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext="136"><mn>136</mn>
</math> millimeters. If <math alttext="cosine upper A equals three fifths"><mi>cos</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>, what is the length, in millimeters, of <math alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b12c5e05-42ce-409b-9044-c1d61da9548b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"34\"><mn>34</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9ce89d5b-4848-4469-9de6-b4f25fd95936","content":"<p><math alttext=\"102\"><mn>102</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"cbf64e03-f7c7-4eeb-95a6-5a9bb315251e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"5f4bf004-8f77-4b9f-b4aa-28dd7dfc76ae","content":"<p><math alttext=\"170\"><mn>170</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9ce89d5b-4848-4469-9de6-b4f25fd95936"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. It's given that in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="triangle upper A upper B upper C"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="angle upper B"><mo>∠</mo><mi>B</mi></math> is a right angle. Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="triangle upper A upper B upper C"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is a right triangle, and&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is the hypotenuse of the triangle. It's also given that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="cosine upper A equals three fifths"><mi>cos</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>. Since the cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse, the ratio of the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5"><mn>5</mn></math>. It follows that the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> can be represented as <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3 a"><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math> and the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> can be represented as <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5 a"><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="a"><mi>a</mi></math> is a constant. The Pythagorean theorem states that the sum of the squares of the length of the legs of a right triangle is equal to the square of the length of its hypotenuse, so it follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B squared plus upper B upper C squared equals upper A upper C squared"><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3 a"><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper B"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5 a"><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper A upper C"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> in this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 3 a right parenthesis squared plus upper B upper C squared equals left parenthesis 5 a right parenthesis squared"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="9 a squared plus upper B upper C squared equals 25 a squared"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="9 a squared"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper B upper C squared equals 16 a squared"><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper B upper C equals 4 a"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi></math>. It follows that the ratio of the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper B upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4"><mn>4</mn></math>. Let&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the length, in millimeters, of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>. It's given that the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper B upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="136"><mn>136</mn></math> millimeters. Since the ratio of the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper B upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4"><mn>4</mn></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction x Over 136 EndFraction equals three fourths"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>136</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="136"><mn>136</mn></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals StartFraction 3 left parenthesis 136 right parenthesis Over 4 EndFraction"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>136</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 102"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>102</mn></math>. Therefore, the length of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="102"><mn>102</mn></math> millimeters.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the scale factor by which the <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="3"><mn>3</mn></math> to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4"><mn>4</mn></math> to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5"><mn>5</mn></math> ratio is multiplied that results in the side lengths of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="triangle upper A upper B upper C"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper B upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>, not the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper C"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>, not the length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="line segment upper A upper B"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5a7e3b46","external_id":"aa7ee350-2124-4fd5-afe1-f10a1f785d05","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"3bdc5476-845d-4c43-9fbc-0988dd0424ef","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In <math alttext=\"triangle upper A upper B upper C\"><mo>&#9651;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper B\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> is a right angle and the length of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn>\n</math> millimeters. If <math alttext=\"cosine upper A equals three fifths\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>, what is the length, in millimeters, of <math alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b12c5e05-42ce-409b-9044-c1d61da9548b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"34\"><mn>34</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9ce89d5b-4848-4469-9de6-b4f25fd95936","content":"<p><math alttext=\"102\"><mn>102</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"cbf64e03-f7c7-4eeb-95a6-5a9bb315251e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"5f4bf004-8f77-4b9f-b4aa-28dd7dfc76ae","content":"<p><math alttext=\"170\"><mn>170</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["9ce89d5b-4848-4469-9de6-b4f25fd95936"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. It's given that in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"triangle upper A upper B upper C\"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"angle upper B\"><mo>∠</mo><mi>B</mi></math> is a right angle. Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"triangle upper A upper B upper C\"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> is a right triangle, and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is the hypotenuse of the triangle. It's also given that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"cosine upper A equals three fifths\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>. Since the cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to the angle to the length of the hypotenuse, the ratio of the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math>. It follows that the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> can be represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3 a\"><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math> and the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> can be represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 a\"><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi></math> is a constant. The Pythagorean theorem states that the sum of the squares of the length of the legs of a right triangle is equal to the square of the length of its hypotenuse, so it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B squared plus upper B upper C squared equals upper A upper C squared\"><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3 a\"><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5 a\"><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> in this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 3 a right parenthesis squared plus upper B upper C squared equals left parenthesis 5 a right parenthesis squared\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 a squared plus upper B upper C squared equals 25 a squared\"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9 a squared\"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> from both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B upper C squared equals 16 a squared\"><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 4 a\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi></math>. It follows that the ratio of the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn></math>. Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi></math> represent the length, in millimeters, of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>. It's given that the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn></math> millimeters. Since the ratio of the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction x Over 136 EndFraction equals three fourths\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>136</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals StartFraction 3 left parenthesis 136 right parenthesis Over 4 EndFraction\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>136</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"x equals 102\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>102</mn></math>. Therefore, the length of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"102\"><mn>102</mn></math> millimeters.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the scale factor by which the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn></math> ratio is multiplied that results in the side lengths of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"triangle upper A upper B upper C\"><mo>△</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>, not the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>, not the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>¯</mo></mover></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017722,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"5a7e3b46","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1755117017722,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"aa7ee350-2124-4fd5-afe1-f10a1f785d05","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"3bdc5476-845d-4c43-9fbc-0988dd0424ef","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In <math alttext=\"triangle upper A upper B upper C\"><mo>&#9651;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math alttext=\"angle upper B\"><mo>&#8736;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> is a right angle and the length of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"line segment upper B upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"136\"><mn>136</mn>\n</math> millimeters. If <math alttext=\"cosine upper A equals three fifths\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>, what is the length, in millimeters, of <math alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>?</p>","keys":["9ce89d5b